WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.615
An upper bound sits at or above every member of a set

00:00:04.615 --> 00:00:11.641
For the open interval from zero to two, two is an upper bound even though it is not a member

00:00:11.641 --> 00:00:14.100
A supremum is the least upper bound.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:25.236
Why can no smaller number be an upper bound? If b lies between zero and two, its midpoint with two still belongs to the interval and exceeds b

00:00:25.236 --> 00:00:29.100
For b at most zero, the member one already defeats it.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.777
Now consider nonnegative rational numbers whose squares are below two

00:00:34.777 --> 00:00:38.717
They form a nonempty set bounded above by two

00:00:38.717 --> 00:00:41.343
Real completeness guarantees a real supremum

00:00:41.343 --> 00:00:47.033
Further algebra shows this boundary squares to two, although it is not rational.

00:00:48.333 --> 00:00:51.898
This illustrates the difference between approximation and existence

00:00:51.898 --> 00:00:58.582
More decimal digits alone do not prove a limit exists in the chosen number system

00:00:58.582 --> 00:01:05.267
Completeness supplies real least upper bounds; the rationals lack the required boundary in this example.
